WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.072
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book two.

2
00:00:05.372 --> 00:00:09.153
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.453 --> 00:00:17.504
Unit fifteen: Feminist Literary Theory. Today you will describe historical marginalization with the present perfect passive.

4
00:00:17.804 --> 00:00:20.444
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:24.946
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.783 --> 00:00:27.086
Feminist theory.

7
00:00:28.083 --> 00:00:32.695
Feminist theory means theory examining gender, power and representation.

8
00:00:33.283 --> 00:00:36.514
Feminist theory reread classic novels from new angles.

9
00:00:37.117 --> 00:00:39.990
Your turn. Say: Feminist theory.

10
00:00:42.833 --> 00:00:44.160
Feminist theory.

11
00:00:44.917 --> 00:00:46.057
Patriarchy.

12
00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:51.070
Patriarchy means a social system in which men hold most power.

13
00:00:51.683 --> 00:00:54.519
The novel critiques patriarchy within the family.

14
00:00:55.117 --> 00:00:58.001
Your turn. Say: Patriarchy.

15
00:01:00.867 --> 00:01:01.972
Patriarchy.

16
00:01:02.717 --> 00:01:03.474
Voice.

17
00:01:04.483 --> 00:01:07.632
Voice means the ability to express one's own perspective.

18
00:01:08.217 --> 00:01:10.495
The heroine finally finds her voice.

19
00:01:11.083 --> 00:01:13.863
Your turn. Say: Voice.

20
00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:17.562
Voice.

21
00:01:18.317 --> 00:01:19.538
Marginalization.

22
00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:24.488
Marginalization means pushing a group to the edges of power or attention.

23
00:01:25.083 --> 00:01:28.128
The marginalization of rural women is a key theme.

24
00:01:28.717 --> 00:01:31.230
Your turn. Say: Marginalization.

25
00:01:34.067 --> 00:01:35.521
Marginalization.

26
00:01:36.283 --> 00:01:37.308
Agency.

27
00:01:38.317 --> 00:01:42.615
Agency means the capacity to act independently and make choices.

28
00:01:43.217 --> 00:01:46.284
The character lacks agency until the final act.

29
00:01:46.883 --> 00:01:49.466
Your turn. Say: Agency.

30
00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:53.220
Agency.

31
00:01:53.983 --> 00:01:55.414
Intersectionality.

32
00:01:56.417 --> 00:02:01.063
Intersectionality means how overlapping identities such as gender and class combine.

33
00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:04.497
Intersectionality explains layered disadvantage.

34
00:02:05.083 --> 00:02:08.305
Your turn. Say: Intersectionality.

35
00:02:11.167 --> 00:02:12.713
Intersectionality.

36
00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:14.718
Representation.

37
00:02:15.717 --> 00:02:19.330
Representation means how groups are portrayed in texts.

38
00:02:19.917 --> 00:02:22.624
Representation in children's books matters.

39
00:02:23.217 --> 00:02:26.380
Your turn. Say: Representation.

40
00:02:29.233 --> 00:02:30.687
Representation.

41
00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:32.451
Empowerment.

42
00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:36.866
Empowerment means the process of gaining power and confidence.

43
00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:39.844
Education has been a path to empowerment.

44
00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:43.009
Your turn. Say: Empowerment.

45
00:02:45.867 --> 00:02:46.972
Empowerment.

46
00:02:47.767 --> 00:02:51.406
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:57.364
Visal, your essay examines women in classic Khmer literature. What's your central argument?

48
00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:08.161
That certain voices have historically been marginalized. Women have often been portrayed as virtuous or tempting, but rarely given agency.

49
00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:11.145
Has that changed in modern writing?

50
00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:20.916
Significantly. In recent decades, women writers have been given more space, and their characters make their own choices.

51
00:03:22.017 --> 00:03:23.923
Where does intersectionality fit in?

52
00:03:25.017 --> 00:03:34.180
It reminds us that a poor rural woman has been excluded in different ways from a wealthy urban one, so we can't treat 'women' as one group.

53
00:03:35.333 --> 00:03:37.263
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:38.117 --> 00:03:41.660
Grammar focus: Passive voice for marginalization.

55
00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:43.217
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:43.917 --> 00:03:47.379
Certain voices have historically been marginalized in literature.

57
00:03:48.283 --> 00:03:51.061
Women writers have recently been given more space.

58
00:03:51.950 --> 00:03:55.111
Rural women have long been excluded from the canon.

59
00:03:56.067 --> 00:04:05.546
Use the present perfect passive for a situation that started in the past and still matters now. Adverbs such as historically, long and often go between 'have' and 'been'.

60
00:04:06.283 --> 00:04:10.735
Be careful! Don't say: Certain voices have historically marginalized.

61
00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:16.464
Say: Certain voices have historically been marginalized.

62
00:04:16.764 --> 00:04:20.157
Present perfect passive: have been + p.p.

63
00:04:20.967 --> 00:04:23.480
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:24.267 --> 00:04:25.802
Rereading the Classics.

65
00:04:26.102 --> 00:04:31.085
Feminist literary theory asks whose experiences have been told and whose have been ignored.

66
00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:37.060
For centuries, women have been represented largely through the eyes of male authors.

67
00:04:38.067 --> 00:04:43.584
As a result, female characters have often been reduced to symbols of virtue, danger or desire.

68
00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:50.686
Feminist critics reread these texts to recover hidden voices and to expose the assumptions of patriarchy.

69
00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:57.624
Intersectional approaches go further, showing that gender interacts with class, ethnicity and age.

70
00:04:58.733 --> 00:05:02.689
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:03.550 --> 00:05:06.188
Women's stories blank ignored for centuries.

72
00:05:06.488 --> 00:05:09.538
A, have been. B, has been. C, have.

73
00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:14.774
The answer is A: have been.

74
00:05:15.917 --> 00:05:18.067
Choose the correct adverb position.

75
00:05:18.367 --> 00:05:26.966
A, Voices historically have been marginalized been.. B, Voices have historically been marginalized.. C, Voices have been marginalized historically been..

76
00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:34.682
The answer is B: Voices have historically been marginalized..

77
00:05:35.817 --> 00:05:38.699
The capacity to make independent choices is blank.

78
00:05:38.999 --> 00:05:42.594
A, agency. B, patriarchy. C, voice.

79
00:05:46.133 --> 00:05:48.005
The answer is A: agency.

80
00:05:49.150 --> 00:05:52.845
How gender, class and ethnicity overlap is called blank.

81
00:05:53.145 --> 00:05:58.377
A, empowerment. B, representation. C, intersectionality.

82
00:06:01.933 --> 00:06:04.548
The answer is C: intersectionality.

83
00:06:05.683 --> 00:06:09.610
According to the reading, how were women mostly represented for centuries?

84
00:06:09.910 --> 00:06:15.410
A, by women themselves. B, through male authors' eyes. C, through statistics.

85
00:06:18.967 --> 00:06:21.988
The answer is B: through male authors' eyes.

86
00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:26.340
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:27.183 --> 00:06:30.297
How can literature give voice to marginalized perspectives?

88
00:06:47.083 --> 00:06:48.572
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:48.872 --> 00:07:24.330
Literature can give voice to marginalized perspectives by allowing readers to see the world through eyes they would never otherwise share. When a story is told by a character who has been ignored, such as a domestic worker or a refugee, readers develop empathy. For example, memoirs by survivors of the Khmer Rouge period have helped younger Cambodians understand their history. Some critics worry that outsiders may speak for others. That is why I believe these stories should be told by the communities themselves.

90
00:07:24.630 --> 00:07:26.340
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:27.250 --> 00:07:32.004
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and passive voice for marginalization.

92
00:07:32.304 --> 00:07:36.860
Today's value is equity. Make room for the voices that are often left unheard.

93
00:07:37.160 --> 00:07:39.940
See you in Unit sixteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:42.997
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

